{%- comment -%}
  Bassface product card — image, title/price row, category row.

  A snippet, not a section: it is the atom the homepage bands are built from and
  the collection, search and related grids will reuse. Dawn's own
  snippets/card-product.liquid is left in place for the stock sections that
  still render it; this does not replace it.

  Accepts:
    product  - a product object. Required. Renders a placeholder when blank, so
               the theme editor still composes with no products assigned.
    ratio    - image aspect ratio, e.g. '3 / 4'. Optional; falls back to 3/4.
               Passed by the CONSUMER per slot, not chosen per product: a card's
               shape belongs to the composition around it (Band B's card is
               square because it is the big slot). The boards use '3 / 4',
               '2 / 3', '1 / 1' and '4 / 5'.
    sizes    - the `sizes` attribute. Pass the band's real column width; the
               default assumes a 4-up grid.
    loading  - 'lazy' (default) or 'eager' for cards above the fold.
    index    - 0-based position among the cards revealing together, which is
               what staggers the entrance across a row. Optional; a lone card
               passes nothing and starts immediately.

  Usage:
    {%- render 'bf-product-card', product: product, ratio: '1 / 1' -%}

  The category line is product.type, uppercased in CSS rather than in the data
  so the merchant's own casing survives in the admin.

  A product's SECOND image, when it has one, is rendered as a hover layer that
  wipes in over the first — see the note on `--alt` in bf-product-card.css. It
  carries data-src rather than src on purpose: bf-product-card.js promotes it to
  a real source only on a pointer that can hover, so a phone never downloads an
  image it has no way to reveal. Cards with a single image simply omit it.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign loading = loading | default: 'lazy'
  assign sizes = sizes | default: '(min-width: 834px) 25vw, 50vw'
  assign alt_image = product.images[1]

  comment
    235ms per slot. The reference staggers its row by 0.2s and then plays the
    whole timeline at a timeScale of 0.85, which works out at 235ms of real
    time — and that is what I measured between cards on the live site, so the
    figure is the observed one rather than the arithmetic one.
  endcomment
  assign reveal_delay = index | default: 0 | times: 235
-%}

{%- capture card_style -%}
  {%- if ratio != blank %}--product-card-ratio: {{ ratio }};{% endif -%}
  {%- if reveal_delay > 0 %}--product-card-reveal-delay: {{ reveal_delay }}ms;{% endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

<bf-product-card
  class="product-card"
  {% if card_style != blank %}
    style="{{ card_style }}"
  {% endif %}
>
  {%- comment -%}
    data-cursor opts this link into the trailing cursor's large labelled state —
    see snippets/bf-cursor.liquid. On the link rather than on the card element
    so the disc appears over exactly the region that is clickable, and no
    wording here: the label is one theme setting rather than a string repeated
    into every card's markup.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <a class="product-card__link" href="{{ product.url | default: '#' }}" data-cursor>
    <div class="product-card__media">
      {%- if product.featured_image -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          alt is empty on purpose: the card is one link whose accessible name is
          the title below, so a described image would have the product read out
          twice.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <img
          class="product-card__image product-card__image--primary"
          src="{{ product.featured_image | image_url: width: 800 }}"
          srcset="
            {{ product.featured_image | image_url: width: 400 }} 400w,
            {{ product.featured_image | image_url: width: 600 }} 600w,
            {{ product.featured_image | image_url: width: 800 }} 800w,
            {{ product.featured_image | image_url: width: 1200 }} 1200w,
            {{ product.featured_image | image_url: width: 1600 }} 1600w
          "
          sizes="{{ sizes }}"
          width="{{ product.featured_image.width }}"
          height="{{ product.featured_image.height }}"
          alt=""
          loading="{{ loading }}"
        >

        {%- if alt_image -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            No src attribute at all until the script arms it. A src-less <img>
            renders nothing and requests nothing, where an empty src would
            resolve against the page URL and fetch the document itself.

            Deliberately no loading="lazy". It looks like the obvious choice and
            it does not work: Chrome never schedules the fetch for a lazy image
            whose src is set from script after layout, so the image sat at
            complete=false forever and the hover never armed. The script arms
            this only when the card scrolls into view, which is what lazy was
            being asked for anyway — so the attribute was buying nothing and
            costing the whole effect.

            fetchpriority stays low: this is never the LCP candidate, even in an
            above-the-fold card.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <img
            class="product-card__image product-card__image--alt"
            data-src="{{ alt_image | image_url: width: 800 }}"
            data-srcset="
              {{ alt_image | image_url: width: 400 }} 400w,
              {{ alt_image | image_url: width: 600 }} 600w,
              {{ alt_image | image_url: width: 800 }} 800w,
              {{ alt_image | image_url: width: 1200 }} 1200w,
              {{ alt_image | image_url: width: 1600 }} 1600w
            "
            sizes="{{ sizes }}"
            width="{{ alt_image.width }}"
            height="{{ alt_image.height }}"
            alt=""
            fetchpriority="low"
          >
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- else -%}
        {{ 'product-1' | placeholder_svg_tag: 'product-card__image product-card__image--primary' }}
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        The entrance curtain. Last in the media box so it paints over both
        images without needing a z-index, and aria-hidden because it is a
        moving rectangle with nothing to announce.

        Rendered whether or not the script runs — it parks off to the right at
        rest, so an unprimed card is simply a card. See the note in
        bf-product-card.css.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <span class="product-card__curtain" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </div>

    <div class="product-card__info">
      {%- comment -%}
        The inner span is the marquee's transform target — see
        bf-product-card.js. It is always rendered, whether or not the title
        overflows; the script measures and skips the ones that fit.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <span class="product-card__title">
        <span class="product-card__title-text">
          {{- product.title | default: 'Product Title' -}}
        </span>
      </span>
      <span class="product-card__price">
        {{- product.price | default: 0 | money -}}
      </span>
    </div>

    {%- if product.type != blank -%}
      <div class="product-card__category">
        <span class="product-card__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="product-card__type">{{ product.type }}</span>
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}
  </a>
</bf-product-card>
